Found by karr-pod-writer while documenting Git.pm for #118 -- writing the
contract down is what made it visible.

App::karr::Git::delete_ref returns 0 in two unrelated situations:

    return 0 unless $repo->reference_exists($ref);   # never existed
    ...
    return 0  if $outcome eq 'failed';               # libgit2 refused

So a caller cannot tell "already gone, nothing to do" from "the delete was
attempted and failed". Verified by reading the sub, not inferred.

Its own CAS sibling does not behave this way: delete_ref_cas dies on that same
class of failure. Every other CAS pair in the file is consistent between the
two variants, which is what makes this look unintentional rather than chosen.

Consequence to weigh: a command that deletes refs and treats 0 as "fine, it was
not there" would silently continue after a real failure. Whether any current
caller does that is the first thing to check -- if one does, this is a live bug
rather than a latent one, and the ticket should be re-prioritised on the spot.

Fix directions, to be decided rather than assumed: distinguish the two (undef
vs 0, or a second return value), or make delete_ref die on real failure like
delete_ref_cas does and let callers that want the soft answer check
reference_exists first. The second is more consistent with the file but changes
behaviour for existing callers, so it needs the call sites reviewed.

Related, same family, cheaper: load_task_ref_with_oid answers a missing task
with (undef, undef), while the read_ref_with_oid it is built on answers a
missing ref with (undef, ''). Both are now documented as they are, but a caller
copying one pattern to the other gets bitten. Worth settling in the same pass.
Die im Ticket offene Frage ist beantwortet: es gibt einen solchen Aufrufer, also ist das ein Live-Bug.

App::karr::Lock::break_lock (Lock.pm:363) verwirft den Rückgabewert:

    next unless $self->git->ref_exists($ref);
    $owner //= $self->git->read_ref($ref);
    $self->git->delete_ref($ref);
    $broke = 1;

$broke wird gesetzt, weil der Ref existierte -- nicht, weil er weg ist. Lehnt
libgit2 den Delete ab (outcome "failed" -> 0), meldet karr unlock trotzdem
"Broke lock on task N (was held by X)" und liefert unter --json broken: true,
waehrend der Lock steht. Cmd/Unlock.pm:131-139 hat nichts anderes, woran es
den Erfolg messen koennte.

Das trifft ausgerechnet den Notausgang: unlock existiert fuer den Fall, dass
ein Halter nicht zurueckkommt. Eine falsche Erfolgsmeldung heisst dort, dass
die Karte fuer alle anderen Agenten gesperrt bleibt und niemand mehr hinsieht,
weil das Werkzeug gesagt hat, es sei erledigt.

Die uebrigen Aufrufer sind unauffaellig: Cmd::Delete geht ueber
delete_task_guarded (CAS, stirbt bei echtem Fehler), delete_refs_under liest
nach der Schleife neu statt ihr zu trauen (Git.pm:2469). break_lock ist der
eine, der 0 verschluckt.

Deshalb hier von medium auf high.
delete_ref now dies on a refused delete (direction b): 1 = removed by this call, 0 = nothing to remove, exception = attempted and refused. Live bug was Lock::break_lock, which set broke=1 off ref_exists and swallowed the 0, so karr unlock announced a broken lock that still stood -- it now surfaces the karr: could not delete ... message instead. The two callers that must survive a refusal say so explicitly: Git::delete_refs (destroy) keeps going and raises every refusal after the loop, Git::replace_board_refs (restore) catches and warns. t/92's 'unguarded on purpose' decision is untouched: that is about the OID guard, not about error handling, and delete_ref is still unguarded. The (undef,undef) vs (undef,'') half is settled as documentation, not behaviour -- the second slot holds a Task in one and text in the other, every caller keys off the OID; both PODs now say so and why. Tests: prove -l t/132-delete-ref-refusal.t (new, 6 subtests, 3 fail against the pre-fix tree incl. the break_lock one); prove -l t/ = 126 files, 888 tests, PASS. Not committed, Changes not touched.
